==Combine==
{{main|NBA Draft Combine}}

The invitation-only [[NBA Draft Combine]] was held in Chicago from May 10 to 15. The on-court element of the combine took place on May 12 and 13. This year, a total of 63 players entered the combine, with the only two alternates that had their invitations be accepted for the event being [[Jaron Blossomgame]] and [[Marcus Lee]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://twitter.com/GoodmanESPN/status/728325025690980353|title=Jeff Goodman on Twitter|work=Twitter|access-date=May 18, 2016|archive-date=September 13, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220913181800/https://twitter.com/GoodmanESPN/status/728325025690980353|url-status=live}}</ref> Furthermore, the only international player that got invited and accepted his invitation this year was [[Zhou Qi]] of the [[Xinjiang Flying Tigers]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.draftexpress.com/article/2016-NBA-Combine-Participant-List-and-Analysis-5472/|title=DraftExpress: 2016 NBA Combine Participant List and Analysis|work=draftexpress.com|access-date=May 18, 2016|archive-date=May 19, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160519054610/http://www.draftexpress.com/article/2016-NBA-Combine-Participant-List-and-Analysis-5472|url-status=live}}</ref> Originally, [[Wayne Selden Jr.]] was to be a participant for the event, but he injured himself before the combine officially began, thus making [[Sheldon McClellan]] from the [[Miami Hurricanes men's basketball]] team his replacement for on-court events.<ref name="sports.yahoo.com">{{cite web|url=https://sports.yahoo.com/news/combine-buzz-194205610.html|title=Combine buzz: Skal Labissiere impresses|date=May 12, 2016|work=Yahoo! Sports|access-date=May 18, 2016|archive-date=May 16, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160516140544/http://sports.yahoo.com/news/combine-buzz-194205610.html|url-status=live}}</ref> [[Buddy Hield]], the consensus national college player of the year for 2015–16, participated only in off-court events; his graduation ceremony at the [[University of Oklahoma]] conflicted with the on-court portion of the combine, and he chose to attend graduation.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://twitter.com/chadfordinsider/status/730424933503926273|title=Chad Ford on Twitter|work=Twitter|access-date=May 18, 2016|archive-date=June 26, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190626225356/https://twitter.com/chadfordinsider/status/730424933503926273|url-status=live}}</ref> [[A. J. Hammons]] also withdrew his name from the draft combine on the day of the on-court events.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://twitter.com/DraftExpress/status/730473272777834496|title=Jonathan Givony|work=Twitter|access-date=May 18, 2016|archive-date=September 13, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220913181800/https://twitter.com/DraftExpress/status/730473272777834496|url-status=live}}</ref>

During the event, sophomore Kentucky and future [[Phoenix Suns]] point guard [[Tyler Ulis]] broke combine records by being the lightest player recorded in draft combine history at 149.2 pounds.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://mediacentral.nba.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/53/2016/05/2016-Draft-Combine-Anthropometric-Results.pdf?cid=nbacomsocial_tw_sf26163323 |title=Archived copy |access-date=May 12, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160605053225/https://mediacentral.nba.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/53/2016/05/2016-Draft-Combine-Anthropometric-Results.pdf?cid=nbacomsocial_tw_sf26163323 |archive-date=June 5, 2016 |url-status=dead |df=mdy-all }}</ref> After the event, nine of the participants that were a part of the combine went back to their respective colleges.{{citation needed|date=May 2016}} However, it was announced just days after the NBA Draft Combine was over that some of the events' measurements would be under review since some prospects were provided with rather questionable results, especially when compared to how they measured up in previous physical events.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.draftexpress.com/article/NBA-League-Office-Draft-Combine-Measurements-Under-Review-5499|title=DraftExpress: NBA League Office: Draft Combine Measurements Under Review|access-date=June 24, 2016|archive-date=September 13, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220913181338/http://www.draftexpress.com/article/NBA-League-Office-Draft-Combine-Measurements-Under-Review-5499/|url-status=live}}</ref>

==Draft lottery==
{{main|NBA draft lottery}}

The NBA conducts an annual lottery to determine the draft order for the teams did not make the playoffs in the preceding season. Every NBA team that missed the NBA playoffs had a chance at winning a top three pick, but teams with worse records had a better chance at winning a top three pick. After the lottery selected the teams that receive a top three pick the other teams receive an NBA draft pick based on their winning percentage from the prior season.  As it is commonplace in the event of identical win–loss records, the NBA performed a random drawing to break the ties on April 15, 2016.<ref name="tiesbroken">{{cite web|url=http://www.nba.com/2016/news/04/15/ties-broken-for-2016-nba-draft-order-of-selection/|title=Ties broken for order of selection in NBA Draft 2016|date=April 15, 2016|work=NBA.com|access-date=May 19, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160521092812/http://www.nba.com/2016/news/04/15/ties-broken-for-2016-nba-draft-order-of-selection|archive-date=May 21, 2016|url-status=dead}}</ref> The table below shows each non-playoff team's chances (based on their record at the end of the NBA season) of receiving picks 1–14.

The 2016 NBA lottery was held on May 17. The [[Philadelphia 76ers]], who had the worst record in the NBA and the highest chance to win the lottery at 26.9% (given the 25% chance to win outright and 1.9% chance that the [[Sacramento Kings]], with whom the 76ers had previously traded for pick-swap rights, would be drawn first), won the lottery. The [[Los Angeles Lakers]] stayed at the second spot, and the [[Brooklyn Nets]] (whose pick was acquired by the [[Boston Celtics]] via an earlier trade) stayed at the third spot.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.espn.com/nba/draft2016/story/_/id/15581021/philadelphia-76ers-win-lottery-pick-first-2016-nba-draft|title=Sixers win NBA lottery for top pick; Lakers get No. 2, Celtics No. 3|work=ESPN|date=May 17, 2016|access-date=May 17, 2016|first=Ohm|last=Youngmisuk|archive-date=May 19, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160519013928/http://espn.go.com/nba/draft2016/story/_/id/15581021/philadelphia-76ers-win-lottery-pick-first-2016-nba-draft|url-status=live}}</ref> As a result, the only team that would have multiple selections in the lottery would be the [[Phoenix Suns]], who hold their own fourth selection, which held the least likely odds of staying exactly where it was at out of all teams in the draft at 9.9%, and the thirteenth selection, which was acquired from the [[Washington Wizards]] earlier in the year and had a 97.8% chance of keeping Washington's selection (either at 96% with Pick 13 or at 1.8% at Pick 14).<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nba.com/suns/news/suns-land-fourth-pick-2016-nba-draft|title=Suns Land Fourth Pick in 2016 NBA Draft|work=NBA|date=May 17, 2016|access-date=May 17, 2016|first=Matt|last=Peterson|archive-date=September 13, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220913181339/https://www.nba.com/suns/news/suns-land-fourth-pick-2016-nba-draft|url-status=live}}</ref> This was the first instance in NBA draft lottery history where every selection remained exactly where it was originally placed before the lottery began, which actually was held by 1.8% likelihood despite having a 1-in-55 chance of it happening due to the lottery selecting only the Top 3 slots.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.cbssports.com/college-basketball/news/infographics-closer-look-at-what-we-learned-from-nba-draft-lottery/|title=INFOGRAPHICS: Closer look at what we learned from NBA Draft Lottery|date=May 18, 2016 |access-date=June 24, 2016|archive-date=September 13, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220913181341/https://www.cbssports.com/college-basketball/news/candid-coaches-should-the-ncaa-tournament-stay-as-is-with-automatic-bids-or-go-to-an-all-at-large-format/xhr/?showTaboola=false|url-status=live}}</ref>

